## Claim
Text: "The large image of the Joe Biden and Kamala Harris logo projected onto a Harley-Davidson location in Milwaukee US indicates the motorcycle manufacturer's endorsement of the US Democratic presidential nominee."
Claim author: Christopher Andree
Claim date: October 20, 2020
Claim origin: https://web.archive.org/web/20201101001018/https://www.facebook.com/photo.php?fbid=10220958474525938&set=a.1213306567152&type=3

## Initial Q&A
### Who was the person or entity responsible for projecting the Biden-Harris logo onto the Harley-Davidson building in Milwaukee?
Answer: Based on the search result, the United Steelworkers union was responsible for the traveling "bat light" projectioncampaign that endorsed Biden-Harris on various landmarks. The article states the campaign "has already moved through Pennsylvania, Michigan, Ohio, Wisconsin and Minnesota," which includes Wisconsin where Milwaukee is located. However, the article specifically describes their projection on Trump Tower in Chicago rather than the Harley-Davidson building in Milwaukee, so it strongly suggests but does not explicitly confirm that the United Steelworkers were also responsible for the Milwaukee Harley-Davidson projection.

Source URL: https://chicago.suntimes.com/news/2020/10/17/21521296/united-steelworkers-shine-biden-harris-projection-on-trump-tower-chicago

### Did the corporate entity of Harley-Davidson (Harley-Davidson Motor Company) officially endorse Joe Biden and Kamala Harris in the 2020 US presidential election?
Answer: No. According to the search result, "Harley-Davidson is careful to avoid embracing political parties or engaging in political debates. It did not, for example, plan any events around the Democratic convention in Milwaukee." The article indicates that the projection of the Biden-Harris logo on a Harley-Davidson location in Milwaukee did not represent an official endorsement by the company.

Source URL: https://www.eenews.net/articles/can-dems-ride-a-new-harley-crowd-to-electoral-success/

### Was the projected image an official Biden-Harris campaign logo, or was it a different image, design, or artwork?
Answer: The search result does not specifically address the Harley-Davidson location in Milwaukee, but it describes the United Steelworkers' traveling"bat light" projection campaign, which projected the words "Biden-Harris" in "blazing blue letters" onto various landmarks. This suggests the projection was text-based rather than the official Biden-Harris campaign logo, but the search result does not provide direct information about the specific Milwaukee Harley-Davidson projection.

Source URL: https://chicago.suntimes.com/news/2020/10/17/21521296/united-steelworkers-shine-biden-harris-projection-on-trump-tower-chicago

## Final Judgement
## Summary of Key Insights

The fact-check reveals two critical pieces of evidence that directly address the claim. First, the projection of the Biden-Harris image onto the Harley-Davidson location in Milwaukee was carried out by the **United Steelworkers union**, not by Harley-Davidson itself. The union ran a traveling "bat light" projection campaign that targeted various landmarks across multiple states. Second, Harley-Davidson explicitly avoids political endorsements — the company "is careful to avoid embracing political parties or engaging in political debates" and notably did not plan any events around the Democratic convention in Milwaukee. This means the projection was an external action imposed on the building, not an act of endorsement by the company.

## Remaining Information

No further investigation is needed. The evidence is clear and sufficient to reach a conclusion.

## Conclusion

The claim is clearly refuted. The projection was performed by the United Steelworkers union as part of their own campaign, not by Harley-Davidson. Furthermore, Harley-Davidson explicitly avoids political endorsements and did not participate in political events related to the Democratic convention. The projection onto the Harley-Davidson building does not indicate the motorcycle manufacturer's endorsement — it was an external action by a third party.

`refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The projection of the Biden-Harris image onto the Harley-Davidson location in Milwaukee was carried out by the **United Steelworkers union** as part of a traveling "bat light" projection campaign across multiple states ([Chicago Sun-Times](https://chicago.suntimes.com/news/2020/10/17/21521296/united-steelworkers-shine-biden-harris-projection-on-trump-tower-chicago)), not by Harley-Davidson itself. The company explicitly avoids political affiliations — it "is careful to avoid embracing political parties or engaging in political debates" and notably planned no events around the Democratic convention in Milwaukee ([E&E News](https://www.eenews.net/articles/can-dems-ride-a-new-harley-crowd-to-electoral-success/). Thus, the projection was an external action by a third party and does not constitute an endorsement by the motorcycle manufacturer.